      <h1>Correction: Umbra and DGX Spark</h1>
      <div class="blog-post-meta">
        <span class="blog-post-date">Originally August 12, 2026 · corrected August 28, 2026</span>
        <span class="blog-post-tag">correction</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="blog-article-lede">The original article combined a reasonable development direction with first-person and performance claims that were not backed by retained, reproducible evidence. It also described planned Umbra behavior too confidently for an unreleased project.</p>

    <div class="blog-body">
      <h2>What remains true</h2>
      <p>Umbra is an early, unreleased attempt at a GUI and terminal interface for local model work. NVIDIA DGX Spark and CUDA are the current target environment. There is no public build, supported hardware matrix, benchmark set, or release date.</p>

      <h2>What has been removed</h2>
      <p>The original post repeated the now-withdrawn claim that a 397B mixture-of-experts model had been run on a 32&nbsp;GB MacBook through custom Metal work. It also stated projected resident-model, activation-capture, and multi-model behavior as though those capabilities had already been measured. Those statements should not be relied on without exact artifacts, commands, raw logs, and repeatable measurements.</p>

      <h2>Current status</h2>
      <p>The <a href="/umbra/">Umbra page</a> is intentionally a design-status page: some details are goals and may change. Any future performance claim should name the hardware, model revision, quantization, runtime, command, prompt or workload, measurement method, and raw result. Until then, “target” and “planned” are the honest words.</p>

